---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import Container from "../components/ui/Container.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../utils/constants";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What the Open Waters site and APIs collect, what they do not, and who operates them."
>
  <Container class="py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="mb-8">Privacy</h1>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
        <p class="text-fg-muted text-xl leading-relaxed">
          Open Waters is run by Open Water Software, LLC. There are no accounts
          here, so there is no profile to build. This page describes what the
          site and the APIs actually see, and what they deliberately do not.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">The website</h2>

        <p>
          Page views are counted with <a href="https://plausible.io/"
            >Plausible</a
          >, which is hosted in the EU and does not use cookies. It records the
          page you asked for, the referrer, and a coarse country, browser and
          device guess, and it does not store an identifier that follows you
          between pages or between visits. No advertising or social network
          scripts load on any page, so nothing here tracks you across other
          sites.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pages with a map load their base map from a third party: the AIS page
          from <a href="https://openfreemap.org/">OpenFreeMap</a>, and the tide,
          chart, and sky pages from <a href="https://carto.com/basemaps/"
            >Carto</a
          > and <a href="https://s2maps.eu/">EOX</a> for Sentinel-2 imagery. Those
          servers see your IP address and the tiles you asked for, under their own
          policies, and nothing else about you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pages outside the AIS map set no cookies. The map at <a
            href="/ais/vessels">/ais/vessels</a
          > stores your light-or-dark choice in a cookie scoped to <code
            >/ais</code
          > once you pick a theme; the <a href="/ais/privacy/"
            >AIS privacy policy</a
          > covers it. If you create an AIS token in the browser, the key is kept
          in your browser's <code>localStorage</code> on your own machine, and clearing
          site data removes it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">The APIs</h2>

        <p>
          The tides API needs no authentication and asks for nothing about you.
          It counts requests per IP address to enforce its published rate
          limits, which is the only thing the address is used for.
        </p>

        <p>
          An AIS token is an Ed25519 keypair generated in your browser, and the
          private key never leaves it. What the AIS service keeps about tokens,
          stations, and API requests, and for how long, is in the <a
            href="/ais/privacy/">Open Waters AIS privacy policy</a
          >. No email address, name or payment detail is required or requested
          for the free tiers. Commercial arrangements are made over email, so
          for those the only record is the correspondence itself.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Vessel positions</h2>

        <p>
          AIS is a public radio broadcast, and the positions Open Waters serves
          are the ones vessels transmit for anyone to hear. Every event names
          the source it came from, whether that is a national open feed, an
          exchange, or a volunteer receiver. Volunteer stations appear under the
          name their operator gave them; the <a href="/ais/privacy/"
            >AIS privacy policy</a
          > covers what a station page shows and how to change it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The track endpoint, <code>/v1/vessels/&#123;mmsi&#125;/track</code>,
          serves the positions the network heard from one vessel; how far back a
          request reaches depends on the token it carries. If you contribute a
          receiver and want to stop, or want its history disassociated from it,
          email us and we will sort it out.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Sharing</h2>

        <p>
          Nothing collected here is sold, and there is nothing collected here
          worth selling. Contributed AIS data is forwarded to the open exchanges
          named in the <a href="/api/ais/">AIS documentation</a>, which is the
          point of contributing it. The website and the tides API run on
          Cloudflare, which sees request traffic as the infrastructure provider.
          The AIS service runs on its own server; its <a href="/ais/privacy/"
            >privacy policy</a
          > names who handles data there.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Questions</h2>

        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with anything
          about this page, including a request to remove something. A person reads
          it.
        </p>

        <p class="text-fg-faint text-sm">
          Open Water Software, LLC · <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
            >{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</MainLayout>
